{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790258774,"nonce":"EByE3WOolyR8kHnnSvwZxUg1CTw=","title":"Python: a literal non-zero divisor is checked for zero","message":"`i % 8` and `x // 4` went through nonzero(), which checks the divisor and raises ZeroDivisionError. That arm makes the enclosing function a raising function, so every inlined call of it is followed by a pending-exception check: bench_method_call (`self.total = self.total + (i % 8)`) loses about 2.5x to PyPy on this alone.\n\nFix: nonzero() returns a non-zero integer literal divisor as it is. `x ** 2` on ints is `x * x` rather than the square-and-multiply loop.","files":null}]}